# Suppressions for the pre-commit publication check.
#
# Each non-comment line is an extended regex matched against
# "<rule-id> <path>:<line>:<text>". Keep every entry as narrow as the thing it
# excuses — a broad pattern here is how a real leak gets waved through, so scope
# to the rule AND the exact literal, never to a whole file or directory.
#
# AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE is Amazon's own documentation placeholder. It appears
# verbatim throughout the public AWS docs and has never been a live credential.
# This repo is the safety scanner, so it needs a realistic key to reason about:
# tests/test_safety_scan.py asserts the scanner rates this value INFO rather than
# blocking it (a marker inside the value itself is decisive evidence of a
# placeholder), and the comments in safety.py explain that rule. Any OTHER
# AKIA-shaped string stays blocked — this entry pins the one literal.
^1a-live-secret .*AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE

# safety.py:95 is a COMMENT explaining that the bare PEM header, with no key body
# after it, is not a leak — which is exactly what the scanner's own rule on line 99
# encodes (`-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----[\r\n]+[A-Za-z0-9+/=\s]{100,}`, requiring
# the body across the newline).
#
# The publish gate's copy of that rule was broken until 2026-08-12: it demanded the
# body on the SAME line, which grep can never match, so real private keys passed.
# Fixing it to the bare header — correct, and what commit-guard.sh always had —
# surfaced this comment. Pinned to the one file and the one rule; any OTHER file in
# this repo carrying a PEM header still fails.
^1a-live-secret skillevaluation/safety\.py:.*BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY
